{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788971078,"nonce":"neR5uFBP3RKBAYNMSNOkk77gu3s=","message":"SCOPING PASS, 2026-09-09. Read-only against `nepo/Source/`, `autorimmer/Source/AutoRimmer/NepoBridge.cs` + `NepoVerbs.cs`, and DESIGN's 2026-09-09 Nepo entry. No bench was touched.\n\nThe premise stands and is the strongest of the four issues in this batch: `nepo-restock` is genuinely unbound, the write path named is the right one, and the \"agent sees dispatches it cannot cause\" observation is exactly right. Five factual corrections, five additions, and a boundary that must be stated before dispatch.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTIONS\n=====================================================================\n\n**1. `RestockOrder.color` is `UnityEngine.Color?`, not a `ColorDef`.**\n`nepo/Source/RestockOrder.cs` — `public class RestockOrder : IExposable` with `ThingDef def; int threshold; int target; bool enabled (= true); ThingDef stuff; Color? color;`. Every field name in the issue is right; only the type of `color` is wrong, and it matters because the bridge's `PubField` helper checks `FieldType` and would refuse a `ColorDef` binding as drift.\n\n**2. `SetRestock`'s last two parameters are OPTIONAL, and that breaks the obvious binding.**\n`NepoGameComponent.SetRestock(ThingDef def, int threshold, int target, bool enabled, ThingDef stuff = null, Color? color = null)`. `AccessTools.Method(comp, \"SetRestock\", new[]{ThingDef,int,int,bool})` returns **null** — `GetMethod` does not match on optionals. Nepo's own `MpBridge.cs` documents this same trap for `RegisterSyncMethod`. Bind the full six-type array. (Moot if the verb only ever calls `NepoSync.SetRestockRule`, which is the ruling below — but the read side still binds `GetRestock`.)\n\n**3. `AllRestocks` is NOT \"a cached list rebuilt on read\".**\n`public IEnumerable\u003cRestockOrder\u003e AllRestocks =\u003e restockOrders.Values;` — an expression-bodied property returning the live `Dictionary\u003cThingDef,RestockOrder\u003e.ValueCollection`. No memo, no rebuild, no allocation, and **zero callers anywhere in Nepo**. It is not a `PawnSafe` Class A/E hazard. The two real hazards, which the spec should name instead:\n  * **Enumerate-while-mutate.** `SetRestock` for a def not yet in the dictionary inserts, so listing and writing in the same pass throws `InvalidOperationException`. Snapshot into our own list first — which is what `NepoBridge.PendingShipments` / `PendingDispatches` already do, and the pattern to copy.\n  * **Live mutable references.** It hands back the real `RestockOrder` objects. Writing `order.threshold` directly would bypass `SetRestock`'s clamp, bypass its `SetMaterialPref` side effect, and bypass MP sync. The verb reads fields off these objects and never assigns to one.\n\n**4. `RunRestockSweep()` is `private`, and the sweep has three guards above it.**\n`NepoGameComponent.GameComponentTick`: `if (!IsNepoGame) return;` → `if (now % 250 != 0) return;` → `if (now % NepoTuning.RestockCheckIntervalTicks == 0 \u0026\u0026 DadAvailable)`, where `RestockCheckIntervalTicks = GenDate.TicksPerHour` (2500). The only public entry is `DevForceRestockSweep()`. So the acceptance bullet \"a rule whose threshold is crossed produces a dispatch visible in `nepo-inbound`\" needs **up to 2,500 ticks of `advance` and a `DadAvailable` colony**, not an immediate read-back. Say so in the acceptance or it reads as a same-call assertion and fails.\n\n**5. \"removing is whatever `SetRestock` does with a zeroed rule (read it, do not invent)\" — read: it does not remove.**\n```\nthreshold = Mathf.Max(0, threshold);\ntarget    = Mathf.Max(threshold + 1, target);\n```\nthen update-or-insert. `SetRestock(def, 0, 0, …)` **stores** `threshold=0, target=1`. There is no removal branch. `RemoveRestock(ThingDef)` exists at `NepoGameComponent.cs` — but it has **zero callers in Nepo**, has **no `NepoSync` wrapper**, and is **not in `MpBridge.RegisterAll`**, so calling it would be an unsynced write and is out of bounds by the same argument that keeps `ScheduleShipment` unbound (DESIGN 2026-09-09). **Ruling: \"remove\" is spelled `enabled:false`, the verb has no delete mode, and the result says why.** The UI itself cannot delete a rule either — the toggle only disables.\n\nAlso correct as written, briefly: `NepoSync.SetRestockRule` exists and is the customize panel's call (`Window_DadCatalog.SyncCustomizationToRule`, and `DrawRestockPanel`'s numeric commit); `WorkGiver_DispatchRestockOrder` exists under that exact name; `requireCommsForRestock` defaults true and its only two call sites are `RunRestockSweep` / `RunSurgeryOrderSweep`; `pendingDispatches` is the sweep's output; the bridge binds `requireCommsForRestock` and nothing else on this surface (grep returns one hit, a doc string in `NepoVerbs.cs`).\n\n=====================================================================\nADDITIONS\n=====================================================================\n\n**A. `SetRestockRule`'s `color` parameter is a STRING, and the encoder is Nepo's.**\n`NepoSync.SetRestockRule(ThingDef def, int threshold, int target, bool enabled, ThingDef stuff, string color)` — six params, **none optional**. `DecodeColor` is private; `NepoSync.EncodeColor(Color?)` is public static and is what the verb must call. This is DESIGN's \"THE PAYLOAD STAYS NEPO'S\" rule (2026-09-09) applied a second time: we do not hand-format `\"r,g,b,a\"`.\n\n**B. There is a second synced entry point, and it is the only thing that CREATES a rule in the UI.**\n`NepoSync.ToggleRestockRule(ThingDef def, int threshold, int target, ThingDef stuff, string color)` — no `enabled` param; it reads current state on the far side. `Window_DadCatalog.DrawRestockPanel`'s number fields only reach the wire `if (comp.GetRestock(e.def) != null)`; the on/off button (`ToggleRestockRule`) is the sole creator. Both are registered at `MpBridge.cs` (`Register(typeof(NepoSync), nameof(NepoSync.SetRestockRule), …)` and `…ToggleRestockRule…`). The verb should use `SetRestockRule` for everything (it is idempotent create-or-update) and DISCLOSE that the window's own create path is `ToggleRestockRule` — but must not reproduce the window's asymmetry, which is a UI accident, not a game rule.\n\n**C. THE VALIDATION IS ALL WIDGET-SIDE — this is the action-model work, and the issue does not mention it.**\nThe backend enforces exactly two things (the clamp above). It does **not** check that the def is orderable, that `stuff` is legal for the def, or that `color` comes from any palette. All three gates live in `Window_DadCatalog` and must be reproduced and cited:\n  * **Catalog membership** — the panel exists only on rows from `CatalogBuilder.OrderableDefs()`, predicate `CatalogBuilder.IsOrderable(ThingDef)`. Already bound in `NepoBridge` as `isOrderable`; reuse it. A rule on a non-orderable def is still swept and priced, reachable only by reflection.\n  * **Stuff** — offered only when `CatalogBuilder.CanCustomizeMaterial(def)` (`def.MadeFromStuff \u0026\u0026 (def.IsArt || def.IsApparel)`), chosen from `CatalogBuilder.AllowedStuffs(def)` = `GenStuff.AllowedStuffsFor(def)`. `Window_DadCatalog.RestockStuff(e)` hard-nulls it otherwise.\n  * **Color** — offered only when `CatalogBuilder.CanCustomizeColor(def)` (`def.IsApparel \u0026\u0026 def.HasComp(typeof(CompColorable))`), from `CatalogBuilder.StylingStationColors()`.\n  * **Numeric range** — `Widgets.TextFieldNumeric(…, 0f, 999999f)` on both fields, then the panel pre-clamps `target = Max(threshold+1, target)` before sending. A player cannot enter a negative or a value past 999,999; the verb refuses those as `bad-args` rather than letting the backend clamp silently.\n\n**D. `SetRestock` ALSO WRITES `materialPrefs`, on every call.** Its last line is `SetMaterialPref(def, stuff, color);`, and `SetMaterialPref` *removes* the pref entry when the choice is default-stuff/no-color. So setting a restock rule silently changes the material a MANUAL `nepo-order` of that def will arrive in. That is a real cross-verb effect and must be published on the result, not discovered.\n\n**E. Rule state is GLOBAL PER SAVE, and the sweep reads one map.**\n`restockOrders` is a `GameComponent` field, scribed with the save; nothing keys on a `Map`. `RunRestockSweep` opens `Map map = Find.AnyPlayerHomeMap;` and returns if null; `ColonyAvailableCount(def)` uses the same. Delivery lands at `Find.AnyPlayerHomeMap ?? Find.Maps.FirstOrDefault()`. With two home maps, \"available\" and the drop are both decided by whichever `AnyPlayerHomeMap` returns — report the map the count came from rather than implying colony-wide.\n\n**F. Bridge mechanics the worker must follow, not reinvent.** `NepoBridge.Resolve()` is bind-once-and-latch (`if (resolved) return; resolved = true;`) and every binding goes through `Bind` / `Getter` / `PubField`, which verify name AND signature AND static-ness so a changed shape disables the verb instead of throwing inside it. `AllRestocks` needs `Getter(comp, \"AllRestocks\", typeof(IEnumerable\u003c\u003e).MakeGenericType(restockType), false, drift)`. `Del\u003cT\u003e` is NOT usable for `SetRestockRule` or `GetRestock` — their signatures mention Nepo types.\n  **Give the restock write its OWN drift tier.** The order surface uses one `orderDriftWhy`; `MpBridge.cs`'s own header argues at length that one flag covering two unrelated features is the bug three sibling bridges shipped. A `restockDriftWhy` + `RestockAvailable` / `RestockUnavailable` pair means a drifted restock write costs the restock verb and nothing else.\n\n**G. The `dad-unavailable` gate is a decision, not a default.** `NepoGate(V, forWrite:true, …)` applies `no-nepo-component` → `gate-unreadable` → `not-a-nepo-colony` → `dad-unavailable`. The restock **panel has no `DadAvailable` check** — a player can edit a rule with Dad gone; what is gated is the *sweep* (`GameComponentTick`'s `\u0026\u0026 DadAvailable`). Enforcing `dad-unavailable` here would fabricate a restriction the player does not have, which is the `auto_arm` / `261f2e9` error facing the other way. **Ruling: pass `forWrite:true` for the component/colony gates but do NOT refuse on `dad-unavailable`; report `dad_available:false` with a sentence saying the rule will be stored and will not sweep until Dad returns.** Record it beside the `requireCommsForRestock` note the file already carries.\n\n**H. Read-back is load-bearing here too.** `SetRestockRule` is `void` and returns silently on `comp == null || def == null`. Re-read with `GetRestock(def)` and compare field-by-field against what was sent, **post-clamp** (`target == Max(threshold+1, target)`), and refuse with a named gate on disagreement — the same shape `nepo-order`'s `order-did-not-take` already has.\n\n=====================================================================\nNO OPEN QUESTION\n=====================================================================\nThis issue raises none, and none was found. `dry_run` follows `NepoOrder`'s shipped shape exactly: `NearMiss(\"dry_run\", \"preview\", \"simulate\", \"plan\")` and `RefuseStray` before any read, every gate runs, then an early return carrying `would:{…}` and `action: NoStamp()`.\n\n=====================================================================\nSIZE AND DEPENDENCY\n=====================================================================\n**Independent of the other three issues in this batch.** It touches no `CompUsable`, no `Ability`, no gizmo. It can be dispatched first or in parallel with all of them.\n\n**Size: two files, ~350–450 lines.** `NepoBridge.cs` +~120 (one type const, `RequireType`, six field bindings, three or four method bindings, its own write tier and drift flag, the accessors). `NepoVerbs.cs` +~250 (one verb: no-args list, single-def read, write with all four widget gates, `dry_run`, read-back, the `materialPrefs` disclosure, the mode block).\n\n**The risky part is not the reflection — it is the four widget gates in addition C.** Every one of them is a `CatalogBuilder` predicate that the backend does not enforce, so a verb that binds `SetRestockRule` and stops there will look finished, pass a smoke test, and hand the agent a rule on a non-orderable def in an illegal stuff that Nepo will happily price and ship. That is the whole of the action-model rule on this issue.\n\n**Second risk: the acceptance's last bullet needs the clock.** The sweep is hourly and `DadAvailable`-gated; a bench check must `advance` at least 2,500 ticks with the threshold genuinely crossed. Written as a same-call assertion it will fail on a correct implementation.","files":null}]}